<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Republic of Austria</h1><p class="st-meta">1945 CE – present · capital Vienna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Second Austrian Republic, restored in 1945 and rebuilt as a neutral, prosperous democracy in the heart of Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in April 1945, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-renner">Karl Renner</a> formed a provisional government and declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria's</a> independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on 27 April 1945, restoring the 1920 constitution. Austria was divided into four Allied occupation zones, with Vienna under four-power control.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Governments were long formed by grand coalitions of the conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-peoples-party">People's Party</a> and the Social Democrats, with social partnership between employers and unions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-state-treaty">State Treaty</a> of 15 May 1955 ended the occupation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> declared permanent neutrality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> became a seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> agencies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waldheim-affair">Waldheim affair</a> of 1986 prompted a reckoning with Austrians' role under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 1995 and adopted the euro in 1999. It remains a federal parliamentary republic of nine states, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> as capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/republic-of-austria.jpg" alt="Republic of Austria" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Bundesministerium für Landesverteidigung · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Austria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1945 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vienna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Karl Renner, Leopold Figl, Bruno Kreisky</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Second Austrian Republic, Austria</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1986&amp;focus=republic-of-austria" aria-label="Republic of Austria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5061 491 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Republic of Austria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5381 747L5229 694M5381 747L5380 747M5381 747L5381 747M5381 747L5381 747"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5380 747h0M5381 747h0M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vienna. <a href="/atlas?year=1986&amp;focus=republic-of-austria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
